AN HONEST NOTE ON WHAT CAN ACTUALLY BE LICENSED

The images in this project were generated by a diffusion model from prompts.
Under current U.S. law this probably means no one holds copyright in the raw
output files themselves.

The requirement that a copyrightable work have a human author was upheld by
the D.C. Circuit in Thaler v. Perlmutter, and the Supreme Court declined to
review that decision on March 2, 2026. The Copyright Office's position is
that prompts operate as instructions conveying unprotectable ideas, because
present-day tools do not give the prompter sufficient control over the
result. No court or agency has yet drawn a line for how much human
contribution is enough; Allen v. Perlmutter may begin to.

So the grant above is made in two registers, and it is worth being clear
about which is which:

  - As to the prompt texts, the written content, and the selection,
    sequencing and arrangement of images into sets and posters, these are
    human-authored and the license operates normally.

  - As to the raw generated image files, the license is better read as a
    statement of intent than as an enforceable restriction. If it turns out
    there is nothing there to license, then nothing here grants or withholds
    anything, and the request stands on its own terms: please don't sell
    these.

Nothing in this file is a representation that any particular element is or
is not protected by copyright, and nothing here is legal advice.


